  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 339 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will build into the mid Mississippi \nvalley and lower Great Lakes Tuesday into Tuesday night. Winds \nwill shift around to the south, however with a cold airmass to \nthe east and south of the area, recycling north, highs will \nstruggle to get out of the lower 40s. A northern stream \nshortwave will track southeast from western North Dakota into \nfar northeastern Nebraska Tuesday night into Wednesday. This \nfeature will force a cold front through the forecast area \nTuesday night. Behind the front, limited cool air advection will\nlead to highs Wednesday in the lower to middle 40s. ATTM, \nlittle to no precipitation will accompany this disturbance and \nfront Tuesday night into Wednesday. The latest NBM ensemble has \na near zero chance of measurable precipitation during this \nperiod over northeastern Nebraska and has the best chances well \noff to the northeast of the forecast area. An active mid level \npattern will continue into Thursday as a secondary shortwave \ntrough dives south from the central Rockies into the southern \nplains. With limited moisture, no precipitation will accompany \nthis feature. Winds will shift back around to the south, then \nwest late Thursday and the latest deterministic GFS soln \nadvertises H85 temps reaching 1 to 4C Thursday afternoon. \nNormally this would equate to highs in the upper 40s, possibly \nnearing 50 in the west. However, the latest NBM ensembles are \nindicative of highs in the middle to upper 40s. For North \nPlatte, the spread in 25% and 75%ile highs for Thursday has \ntightened up tonight compared to previous runs and now ranges \nfrom 41 to 47 degrees. Ridging will build into the western third\nof the CONUS late in the week allowing warmer air to push east \nof the Rockies onto the high plains. Highs Friday and Saturday \nwill trend upward into the lower 50s with some mid 50s possible \nin far SW Nebraska. For reference, average highs for North \nPlatte and Valentine Friday are 48 and 45 degrees respectively, \nso forecast highs will run 5 to 10 degrees above climo for \nFriday and Saturday.",
